WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.870 --> 00:00:13.030 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  hello and welcome to this virtual tour  of the rayleigh windmill a tourist and  educational attraction boasting a museum  exhibition space and many interesting  facts and displays showing the history  of rayleigh and the rayleigh mount  the rayleigh windmill also serves as a  popular venue for weddings and other  celebrations  [Music]  the windmill can be found within the  rochford district which is located in  essex the rochford district boasts an  area of 65 square miles and offers  plenty of character and natural beauty  through its wonderful countryside and  miles of coastline  the district is enriched with a  tremendous amount of charm and history  and the windmill perfectly encapsulates  these traits  [Music]  rayleigh itself is an historic market  town and the main center of population  within the districts  the town has retained much of its  traditional character and provides a  vibrant town center  the windmill can be found along  bellingham lane a side road from the  high street  the windmill is a grade two listed  building and this beautiful local  landmark was first built in 1809 it  spent much of the late 20th century  disused but has since been reborn as a  result of extensive restoration work  carried out in 2006 allowing it to  reopen to the public  these restoration works also allowed the  windmill to host celebrations including  weddings with its majestic sales and  picturesque garden it offers a stunning  backdrop for photographs and is perfect  for those looking for a venue full of  charm and romance  [Music]  [Applause]  the windmill is owned and managed by  rochford district council  the red brick tower stands at 55 feet  tall and with the cap and sails included  the windmill stands at 67 feet tall  making it the tallest in essex the sails  on the windmill are around 40 feet in  length and were removed in october 2017  for essential maintenance before being  restored  as you move up each floor reduces in  area as the tower tapers towards the top  of its six floors only the first four  are accessible to the public  adjacent to the windmill is the windmill  garden which provides a lovely and  peaceful area for visitors  [Music]  a commemorative sculpture can be found  within the garden which highlights the  inner workings of the windmill and is  almost seven feet tall weighing  approximately half a ton  the sculpture was designed in  collaboration with local school children  from across the district and was  unveiled in 2009 as part of a  celebration of the 200th anniversary of  the rayleigh windmill  originally when the windmill was  operational each floor had a separate  and important part to play in the flower  producing process  [Music]  the ground floor would mainly be used  for the management of the business and  is where the sacks of grain would have  been received and weighed  additionally administrative tasks  crucial for business would have been  performed here  [Music]  [Laughter]  once received and weighed the sacks of  grain would be winched to the top of the  mill with a hoist and one-way trapdoors  passing through each floor to the top of  the mill  moving up the windmill the first floor  is where the miller worked and  controlled each element of production  all the energy that was created by the  sails would have been brought to this  floor as it would turn the great spur  which was a large wheel that would turn  above the miller's head  this great spur wheel had gears known as  stone nuts which were used to operate  all the various pieces of machinery  present  the next floor is where you would find  three sets of stones which were crucial  for the production of produce at the  windmill utilizing the power coming from  the work of the miller on the floor  below each stone rotated over a static  bed stone  [Music]  the third floor was known as the bin  floor as the grain would have been fed  from the sacks in the floor above into  bins from here the grain would then feed  through into the stones on the second  floor below by passing through the  hopper  continuing upwards the fourth floor was  for the short-term storing of the sacks  of grain whilst they waited to fill the  bins below and then start the whole  process  above this the fifth floor was an area  with a very low ceiling and was known as  the dust floor  the reason for having a dust floor  located here was to minimize the risk of  unwanted debris entering the process by  creating a barrier between the moving  cap and the mill  and finally at the very top of the  windmill we have the cap located on the  sixth floor which also houses the brake  wheel this receives power directly from  the sails and in turn uses the gearing  to send the power to the great spur  wheel with a miller on the second floor  of the windmill  at the rear of the cap is the fan tail  which rotated the cap and sails into the  wind  the windmill cap would be particularly  vulnerable to high wind damage which  would make access for regular  maintenance a very daunting and  dangerous prospect  what a fascinating insight into the  history of the windmill and how this  iconic local building functioned when it  was in operation  now we would love to take a look at what  you can find at the windmill today as  there are many delightful and  interesting things to come along and see  you will be greeted by the wonderful  rayleigh windmill volunteers often in  costume upon your arrival  the volunteers are the foundation upon  which the entire experience is built  their dedication and extensive knowledge  on the history of the windmill is  unparalleled and a highlight for all  visiting  the ground floor is where you can relive  some of the highlights and the history  of the windmill through both the  extensive knowledge of the volunteers  and the storyboards that you can find  displayed across the room  [Music]  you can find further detail on each of  these storyboards by visiting  www.rochford.gov  dot uk forward slash windmill  the ground floor also has a tv screen  for short presentations and offers  footage of the upper floors for those  who are unable to access them  for younger visitors the ground floor  has an activity table with lots of fun  pieces of equipment for them to enjoy  there is a demonstration from the  volunteers on a set of hand-operated  corn stones and how this would have been  used to turn grain into flour when the  windmill was operational  the volunteers also have some  fascinating stories that are always  popular amongst visitors  moving upstairs the first floor is the  windmills museum floor  the items on display are very household  and domestic based with many items being  donated by residents from the local area  the first floor includes many different  exhibits and artifacts offering  delightful insight into some local  history and providing a glimpse into  what life was like for the residents of  raleigh and neighborhoods during the  early years of the 20th century  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]  items from both world wars can be found  on display as well as everyday household  items from this time period  [Music]  some of the pieces you will find include  a collection of pre-decimal coins  charity pins including an early poppy  made of silk and a vast array of  historical photos and paper cuttings  showing the life and times of rayleigh  down the years  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]  upstairs the second floor of the  windmill now serves mainly as an  exhibition floor for local artists and  community organizations artists are  invited to utilize the space at the  windmill for a few weeks as an  opportunity to showcase their work and  talent to all who visit  during the season we have many different  artists offering a wide variety of art  and technique and the artists remain  present during their exhibition to speak  to visitors to explain their art and  their artistic process also on this  floor thanks to various donations we  have an exhibition displaying a number  of early rural craft items this display  was prompted from the acquisition of a  sac with the logo of the rayleigh  windmill  it was in poor condition as it had been  stored at the back of a barn for many  years but fortunately we were able to  have it restored  [Music]  thelma past field was born at the mill  as her father worked as a wagon driver  for thomas brown the owner of the  windmill at the time  it was a donation from thelma's sister  that paid for the saks restoration and  resulted in the purchase of related  items in order to create a display  reflecting the bygone era of rayleigh's  agricultural and rural past  [Music]  [Music]  finally the third floor is the last that  is still accessible to the public at the  windmill and therefore is the highest we  can go  [Music]  this floor is dedicated to the rayleigh  mount and its history which is in an  area adjacent to the windmill owned by  the national trust the mount was  formerly the location of rayleigh castle  which was a muttoned bailey castle  the castle was built around 1070 and was  referenced in the doomsday book  published in 1086  the castle was built and occupied by  swain who had inherited the land from  his father fitzway mark  the castle was located here at the mount  as it was a natural vantage point for  the entire area this is shown from the  wonderful views you can find from the  third floor of the windmill  these views overlook rayleigh and even  offer sights as far as south end south  wooden ferris and the lachinden water  tower  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]  a truly iconic local landmark the  rayleigh windmill has been standing tall  and proud for over 200 years  there is so much more to the history of  this wonderful windmill the rayleigh  windmill has been awarded the  accreditation of quality assured visitor  attraction from visit england and is a  national winner of the heritage and  museum awards for customer service we  would love for you to come and visit us  when we are open and meet our lovely  volunteers  further information including details on  the windmills beautiful wedding  ceremonies can be found at  dot www.rochford.gov forward slash  windmill or by calling zero one seven  zero two three one eight one two zero  [Music]  you

